\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Report on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

How Elected Judges Respond to the Racial Composition of their Constituencies
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper examines how the race of the defandant's community is related
 to the incarceration and the sentence length.
 The study takes advantage of the variation in the judicial selection system
 to estimate the relationship between the percent of a district's population
 that is of the defendant's race and the length of that defendant's sentence.
 Judges who are elected serve as the treatment group, while judges who are
 appointed serve as the control group.
 The authors claim that elected judges are more responsive to the racial
 composition of their constituents because they face reelection.
 This will, in turn, directly affect the sentencing decisions of these judges.
 Results indicate that African Americans facing an elected judge receive
 shorter sentences as the percentage of the African American population
 in their district rises, while the probability of incarceration is unaffected.
 The relationship is insignifiant for defendants of other races or when
 the defendant faces an appointed judge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Comments and Concerns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What is the identifying assumption? No where in the paper is the identifying
 assumption explicitly discussed.
 The authors explain that they are taking advantage of the variation in
 the judicial selection system.
 However, states determined the method of their judicial selection years
 prior, with some states even deciding to amend their constitutions in order
 to change the method.
 it is difficult to verify if this variation is truly exogenous.
 Most importantly, how do the authors account for the states that opt for
 a hybrid of elective and appointive positions, such as Colorado?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to estimate the effect racial composition of a district has on
 sentence length, the authors compare judicial districts in which judges
 are elected (treatment group) to judicial districts in which judges are
 appointed (controlled).
 By doing this, they implicitly assume that all observable and unobservable
 characteristics of judicial districts are the same except for the method
 of judicial selection.
 Yet, the authors readily state that counties with elected judges have higher
 mean population of blacks, and elected judges handle more black defendants.
 For this reason, I believe the estimates corresponding to the African American
 population are biased.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The data on court cases only account for around a third of U.S.
 population, and about a half of all crime in the U.S.
 So, the remaining two-thirds of the population is not even represented
 in the data.
 In addition, it is not unreasonable to believe that individuals in represented
 counties could have easily committed crimes in any of the excluded judicial
 districts.
 To sum up, not having data for all of the U.S.
 has the potential to bias the estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What is the explanation for not including judicial district and/or region
 fixed effects? Various counties and/or regions in the U.S.
 probably have different crime rates, conviction rates, philosophies, political
 biases, etc.
 that could affect the incarceration rates and lengths of sentences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What is the advantage of estimating a two-stage Heckman regression? Some
 of the control variables used in the first stage estimation, but excluded
 in the second stage, could in fact influence the second stage.
 Moreover, the authors along account for trials overseen by judges, and
 it seems trials by jury are simply overlooked.
 The probability of conviction surely differs between trials by jury and
 those simply oversoon by a judge, which, in turn, could lead to differening
 probabilities of incarceration.
\end_layout

